I.	Linear Search
Definition: Linear Search is a simple searching algorithm that checks each element of an array sequentially 
until the required element is found or the end of the array is reached.
Unlike Binary Search, Linear Search does not require the array to be sorted. 
It starts from the first element and compares each element with the target value until a match is found.
Time Complexity
Best Case: O(1)
Average Case: O(n)
Worst Case: O(n)
Auxiliary Space
Linear Search requires O(1) auxiliary space because it uses only a constant amount of additional memory for variables such as the loop counter and search key.
Conclusion
Linear Search is a simple and easy-to-implement searching algorithm. 
It can be used on both sorted and unsorted arrays and does not require any preprocessing of the data.
However, its O(n) average and worst-case time complexity makes it less efficient than Binary Search for large sorted datasets. 
It is suitable for small datasets and situations where the data is unsorted.




